Kubernetes Cluster Upgrade Using kubeadm (Step-by-Step | Easy Explanation) 🚀

Upgrading a Kubernetes cluster doesn’t have to be scary.
If your cluster is created using kubeadm, follow this safe and production-friendly approach 👇

🔹 Why Upgrade Kubernetes?

 Security patches
 Performance improvements
 New features
Stay within supported versions

kubeadm Upgrade Flow (Simple & Practical)

1. Check Current Version
kubectl version
kubectl get nodes

2. Take Backup (Very Important!)

Backup etcd

Backup /etc/kubernetes/

This is your rollback plan.

3. Upgrade Control Plane (Master Node First)

🔸 Upgrade kubeadm

apt update
apt install -y kubeadm=<new-version>


🔸 Check upgrade plan

kubeadm upgrade plan


🔸 Apply upgrade

kubeadm upgrade apply v<new-version>

Upgrade kubelet & kubectl

apt install -y kubelet=<new-version> kubectl=<new-version>
systemctl restart kubelet


 Control Plane upgraded without downtime.

4. Upgrade Worker Nodes (One by One)

🔸 Drain node safely

kubectl drain <node-name> --ignore-daemonsets


🔸 Upgrade kubeadm & kubelet
🔸 Restart kubelet
🔸 Bring node back

kubectl uncordon <node-name>
 Repeat for each worker node.

 Verify Cluster Health
bash
Copy code
kubectl get nodes
kubectl get pods -A

All nodes should be Ready and pods Running 

 Golden Rules

 Control Plane first
 Workers later
 One minor version at a time
 One node at a time = Zero downtime

🔥 If you’re learning Kubernetes / DevOps / SRE, mastering cluster upgrades is a must-have production skill.
